C-6 two-scale analysis. Exact finite-L lower edge (closed form, verified below):
  w-_L(m) = 4 sin(pi m/L) sin(pi(N+1-m)/L) for m<=N,  4 sin(pi m/L) sin(pi(m-N+1)/L) for m>N
  small-q scaling form: w- ~= q |q - 2k_F(L)|,  k_F(L) ~ pi L^{-1/2}: two scales q and k_F(L).
  Path q_L = 2pi m/L with m = max(1, round(c L^{1-a})); successive slopes dlog w/dlog q_L over L=10^4..10^12:
  a=0.25 c=0.05: slopes 2.8664, 1.3272, 1.8630, 1.9608   (m/N at largest L = 50)  predicted 2.0000
  a=0.50 c=0.05: slopes 1.9999, 2.0000, 2.0000, 2.0000   (m/N at largest L = 0.05)  predicted 2.0000
  a=0.50 c=3.00: slopes 2.0030, 2.0004, 2.0000, 2.0000   (m/N at largest L = 3)  predicted 2.0000
  a=0.50 c=1.00: slopes 2.9999, 3.0000, 3.0000, 3.0000   (m/N at largest L = 1)  predicted 2.0000
  a=0.60 c=0.05: slopes 1.8397, 1.8203, 1.8332, 1.8327   (m/N at largest L = 0.00316)  predicted 1.8333
  a=0.75 c=1.00: slopes 1.6478, 1.6579, 1.6645, 1.6662   (m/N at largest L = 0.001)  predicted 1.6667
  a=0.90 c=1.00: slopes 1.5272, 1.5475, 1.5623, 1.5568   (m/N at largest L = 1.6e-05)  predicted 1.5556
  a=1.00 c=1.00: slopes 1.4980, 1.4998, 1.5000, 1.5000   (m/N at largest L = 1e-06)  predicted 1.5000
  a=1/2 with c ~ 1 (m ~ N, i.e. q ~ 2k_F(L)) rides the moving soft line; exponent then not 2 (see c=1.0 row).
  finite-L closed form vs brute force (C-6, L=1000,4096,10000, all m<=L/2): mismatches=0
General N ~ L^alpha: w1 = 4 sin(pi/L) sin(pi N/L) ~ 4 pi^2 L^{alpha-2} -> z_P' = 2-alpha (alpha<1); C-6 (alpha=1/2) z_P'=3/2 while z_P=2.

